--- body ---
## Context

During `/gaze` round 2 on `search-works-for-zotero` PR #553 (2026-09-13),
phases 2-4 and 6 spawn read-only reviewer Agents with cwd pinned to the
isolated review worktree `$primary_root/.claude/worktrees/review-553`,
exactly as `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` phase 1 sets up and phases 2-4/6 require
("Do **not** give these agents `isolation: "worktree"`... cwd is **pinned to
the existing review worktree**"). In this run every one of those agents had
its git operations refused by the worktree-identity guard — including
read-only ones — and fell back to running inside the caller's (the
orchestrating team-lead's) own worktree instead. The isolation phase 1 exists
to provide gave none, silently: no error, no flag, just a quietly wrong cwd.
The run still produced a correct verdict (the internal battery caught a real
liveness bug and four wrong numbers), so this did not corrupt that result,
but the isolation guarantee `/gaze` documents and depends on did not hold,
and a future run relying on it for actual containment (not just correctness)
would not be warned.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — phase 1 (`## 1. Setup`, isolation setup shell
  block) creates the review worktree; phases 2-4 and 6 pin reviewer Agent cwd
  to it and rely on "the same worktree-identity check as every worktree: an
  Edit/Write is allowed when the acting process is physically inside that
  worktree, denied otherwise" (phase 1 comment, ~line 150).
- `scripts/pretooluse-worktree-path-guard.sh` — the identity guard itself; it
  refused git operations naming `review-553`, including read-only ones.

## Actions

1. Reproduce: spawn a read-only Agent with cwd pinned to a
   `.claude/worktrees/review-<n>` tree (via the `Agent` tool's cwd pinning,
   the same mechanism `/gaze` uses — not `EnterWorktree`) and have it run a
   read-only git command (`git status`, `git log`) naming that path. Confirm
   whether the guard denies it or misroutes it to the caller's worktree.
2. If reproduced, diagnose why the guard's "acting process is physically
   inside that worktree" check fails for an Agent-spawned subagent whose cwd
   was pinned this way — the guard likely keys off session-level worktree
   state that a subagent's pinned cwd does not set, rather than the
   subagent's actual process cwd.
3. Fix the guard (or the pinning mechanism) so a read-only reviewer Agent
   genuinely confined to `review-<pr-number>` is recognized as inside it, and
   its git operations succeed there instead of silently falling back to the
   caller's own worktree.

## Test

- Red first: spawn a read-only Agent cwd-pinned to an isolated review
  worktree containing a marker absent from the caller's worktree (e.g. a
  distinct file or branch); have it read that marker. Today it should
  correctly find the marker only if the fallback lands in the caller's tree
  instead — asserting on the actual git state read, not on the agent's own
  claim of where it ran, is what makes this test discriminate the bug from
  a fix.

## Verification

- [ ] A cwd-pinned reviewer Agent's git operations resolve against its own
      pinned worktree, confirmed by a distinguishing marker.
- [ ] The fix is exercised by an actual `/gaze` run (or a scripted
      equivalent) showing zero guard refusals for the pinned cwd.

## Invariants

- The fallback-to-caller's-worktree behavior must not silently succeed; if
  isolation cannot be provided, `/gaze` should say so loudly (a
  `PANEL-INTEGRITY`-style flag), not proceed silently as if isolated.
- No change to the guard's actual purpose (preventing cross-worktree writes)
  — only to recognizing a legitimately pinned reviewer as inside its own
  tree.

## Exit criteria

A read-only reviewer Agent whose cwd is pinned to
`$primary_root/.claude/worktrees/review-<pr-number>` can perform git
operations (including read-only ones) against that worktree without the
identity guard refusing them and without silently falling back to the
caller's tree — verified by an actual `/gaze` run showing no isolation
fallback.
